Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether the condition $f(A)+f(B)+f(C)+f(D)=0$ for every square $ABCD$ forces $f \equiv 0$.

**Answer: Yes, $f(P)=0$ for all points $P$.**

**Proof.**

Identify the plane with $\mathbb{R}^2$. Fix an arbitrary point $(x,y)$ and a real number $t \neq 0$.

**Step 1: Two families of squares.**

The four points $(x+t,\, y),\; (x,\, y+t),\; (x-t,\, y),\; (x,\, y-t)$ form a square (a "diamond" with diagonals along the axes, side length $t\sqrt{2}$). The hypothesis gives:

$$f(x+t,y)+f(x,y+t)+f(x-t,y)+f(x,y-t)=0. \tag{1}$$

The four points $(x+t,y+t),\;(x-t,y+t),\;(x-t,y-t),\;(x+t,y-t)$ form a square (axis-aligned, side length $2t$). The hypothesis gives:

$$f(x+t,y+t)+f(x-t,y+t)+f(x-t,y-t)+f(x+t,y-t)=0. \tag{S}$$

**Step 2: Write equation (1) at four neighboring centers.**

Apply (1) with center $(x+t,y)$ and parameter $t$:
$$f(x+2t,y)+f(x+t,y+t)+f(x,y)+f(x+t,y-t)=0. \tag{2}$$

Apply (1) with center $(x-t,y)$ and parameter $t$:
$$f(x,y)+f(x-t,y+t)+f(x-2t,y)+f(x-t,y-t)=0. \tag{3}$$

Apply (1) with center $(x,y+t)$ and parameter $t$:
$$f(x+t,y+t)+f(x-t,y+t)+f(x,y+2t)+f(x,y)=0. \tag{4}$$

Apply (1) with center $(x,y-t)$ and parameter $t$:
$$f(x+t,y-t)+f(x-t,y-t)+f(x,y)+f(x,y-2t)=0. \tag{5}$$

**Step 3: Add equations (2)+(3)+(4)+(5).**

Collecting terms:
- $f(x+2t,y)+f(x-2t,y)+f(x,y+2t)+f(x,y-2t)$: this is exactly equation (1) with parameter $2t$ (valid since $2t\neq 0$), so it equals $0$.
- $f(x,y)$ appears in all four equations: contributes $4f(x,y)$.
- $f(x+t,y+t)$ appears in (2) and (4): contributes $2f(x+t,y+t)$.
- $f(x+t,y-t)$ appears in (2) and (5): contributes $2f(x+t,y-t)$.
- $f(x-t,y+t)$ appears in (3) and (4): contributes $2f(x-t,y+t)$.
- $f(x-t,y-t)$ appears in (3) and (5): contributes $2f(x-t,y-t)$.

The sum of the last four contributions is $2\bigl[f(x+t,y+t)+f(x-t,y+t)+f(x-t,y-t)+f(x+t,y-t)\bigr]$, which equals $2\cdot 0 = 0$ by equation (S).

Therefore:
$$0 + 4f(x,y) + 0 = 0,$$
which gives $f(x,y) = 0$.

**Step 4: Conclusion.**

The point $(x,y)$ was arbitrary and $t\neq 0$ was arbitrary (any nonzero $t$ works). All squares used are non-degenerate. Therefore $f(P)=0$ for every point $P$ in the plane. $\blacksquare$

